+# Keep pickup reminders on SMS
+
+For volunteers considering a cheaper channel for pickup reminders. Keep SMS: the email trial saved messaging costs
+but coincided with more missed pickups and wasted fresh food.
+
+## Context
+
+The pantry tried email pickup reminders from April through June 2025. According to the provider's report cited in
+[issue 52](../../notes/issue-52.md), 34% of reminder emails were never opened. Missed pickups rose from 6% to 13% of
+pantry orders. Each missed pickup leaves a packed bag, whose fresh food is discarded at closing time.
+
+The pantry lead reported that many members share one phone in the family and do not check email, but do see SMS.
+The pantry switched back to SMS on July 1, 2025; missed pickups returned to 6% by August. When email was proposed
+again in issue 52, it was the third such proposal that year. The maintainer reaffirmed keeping SMS.
+
+The choice weighs messaging costs against reaching members in time to collect their pantry orders and avoiding
+food waste.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Email**: free to send, saving the reported €40 monthly SMS cost. Rejected because the trial left 34% of reminders
+  unopened and missed pickups rose to 13%; it also failed to reach members who do not check email.
+- **SMS**: costs the reported €40 per month, but reaches members on the phone they use. Chosen after missed pickups
+  returned to 6% following its restoration.
+
+## Decision
+
+Keep pickup reminders on SMS. The observed pickup rates and members' access to messages justify the monthly cost:
+email's savings came with more uncollected pantry orders and discarded fresh food.
+
+## Consequences
+
+The pantry continues paying for SMS rather than taking email's messaging savings. Members receive reminders through
+the channel the pantry lead reports they see. Future proposals to change channels should address the trial's
+unopened reminders, missed pickups and food waste, as well as the cost of sending messages.
